when the pandemic hit companies either  blew up or they got destroyed companies  that benefited from people staying at  home like zoom google amazon and  facebook went to the moon meanwhile  restaurants airlines cruise lines and  hotels went into the abyss and many of  them even filed for bankruptcy one of  the biggest beneficiaries of the  movement towards stereotome products was  an exercise equipment company called  peloton almost overnight they became  iconic for making high-end exercise  bikes and treadmills and their stock  boomed almost 10x from the pandemic lows  but ever since the start of 2021 peloton  hasn't been doing so well they've had to  recall a couple of products face a few  pr nightmares slash the prices of their  products by hundreds of dollars watch  their products oversaturate secondhand  markets lose their ceo and burn more  money than ever before as you would  guess the stock market hasn't been very  kind to this lackluster performance and  the stock has sold off 86 from the peak  so here is the rise and fall of one of  the biggest fads in the fitness industry  taking a look back the story of peloton  circles back to a man named john foley  we don't know too much about jon's  background other than the fact that he  seems like a traditional businessman who  climbed a corporate ladder he got  prestigious degrees in engineering and  business from georgia tech and harvard  respectively and through decades of work  he was able to become the president of  e-commerce at barnes noble at this point  most people would probably just try to  work up to the c-suite work for another  10-20 years and then retire comfortably  but it looks like jon had an  entrepreneurial urge that he had never  tended to so in 2012 at age 40 john made  the fateful decision to quit his job and  give business a shot he didn't want to  do it alone though so he convinced a  bunch of executive friends to take the  plunge with him this included graham  stanton hisokushi tom cortese and yanni  feng and given all of their connections  and personal wealth itself it wasn't  difficult to raise money  just one month after starting the  company they were able to raise four  hundred thousand dollars and by the end  of their first year they were able to  raise another 3.5 million dollars since  then peloton has managed to raise over  one billion dollars from their private  markets but anyway with their first few  millions they started developing their  flagship product which is their exercise  bike the idea was to allow people to  enjoy a high-end cycling class from the  comfort of their homes  each bike comes with a massive screen  that's capable of live streaming  workouts from dozens of their personal  trainers the product wasn't anything  game changing per se but it provided a  unique twist on a classic product and  usually that's all you need  people often get stuck up on trying to  find the next big idea when the best  idea is actually just to noticeably  improve something that already exists  anyway peloton turned to kickstarter to  launch their first bike with the goal of  raising 250 000  they were only able to garner a total of  297 backers but given that each bike  cost over a thousand dollars they were  able to beat their goal and raise 307  thousand dollars peloton would turn  around and release their bikes to the  general public in 2014 and with that  peloton was in business  over the next few years peloton slowly  grew a core base of hardcore users  initially their products mainly appealed  to real biking enthusiasts who wanted a  solid alternative to biking outside or  attending a real cycling class but with  the time their demographic would shift  closer and closer to everyday people in  may of 2018 peloton would attempt to  expand their audience by targeting  enthusiast runners as well with the  launch of the peloton tread plus and the  tread plus carried an even more bougie  price on their bikes starting at 4 300  it should also be noted that if you want  access to all of palton's classes and  live streams you have to get their  all-access subscription which costs 39  per month or 468 per year itself for  those prices i'm pretty sure it would be  way cheaper to get a gym membership and  just attend cycling classes in person if  a gym membership costs 30 per month it  would take nearly 12 years to pay the 4  300 that it would cost to just get the  tread plus despite these crazy prices  for people who highly value the  livestream classes and the convenience  there was no better at home alternative  also with time their high price tag  started to become more of an advantage  than a hindrance similar to other  high-end brands like apple and louis  vuitton peloton's high prices gave them  exclusivity and turned their products  into status symbols  if you were able to spend thousands on  an exercise bike and hundreds on a  subscription every year you clearly had  money in my opinion if you don't know  what peloton means you probably  shouldn't be getting their bikes and  this is how it mostly was till 2020 but  once the pandemic hit a large portion of  white-collar workers decided that it was  a great idea to buy some high-end  equipment to start exercising on and  over the next few quarters peloton's  revenue numbers would hit new all-time  highs and they would even post a few  profitable quarters but while peloton  was doing better than ever before  financially they were concurrently  dealing with the slew of problems in the  background  one aspect that most youtubers are  extremely careful of is avoiding  copyrighted music  even just a couple of seconds of  copyrighted music can get an entire  video demonetized unfortunately it looks  like peloton didn't have any youtubers  on their design team because they filled  their workout videos with copyrighted  music and in march of 2019 the national  music publishers association would hit  peloton with a 150 million lawsuit  peloton instantly responded by removing  the copyrighted music from all of their  courses and even removing some courses  that depended on the music most peloton  users weren't exactly happy with this  given that copyrighted music is  generally way better than  non-copyrighted music but peloton's  customers weren't the only ones that  were unhappy in september of 2019 the  music association would double their  allegations on peloton claiming a total  of 300 million dollars peloton did end  up settling for an undisclosed amount  that was likely lower than 300 million  dollars but i doubt that this was a very  good experience for peloton shortly  after they would find themselves in yet  another controversy with a commercial  called a gift that gives back the plot  of the commercial is a husband buying  his wife a peloton bike and the wife  becoming a lot more fit and athletic  over the next year most people would  just dismiss the commercial as another  fitness ad and not think much about it  but a select group of people felt that  peloton was promoting body shaming and  that the plot of the commercial implied  their husband was dissatisfied with the  wise physical appearance honestly it's  just a traditional fitness commercial  but the like to dislike ratio shows how  divided people were on the advertisement  despite all these controversies peloton  sales continued to rise through the  start of the pandemic but this led to  its own issues  peloton couldn't keep up with the  massive influx of new orders and many  customers had waiting times that  stretched several months this led to  many customers reconsidering their  orders and cancelling them a few months  into the weight while these growing  pains were likely annoying to deal with  it soon became clear that peloton had  much more deep rooted problems  the truth is that a significant portion  of the people who went ahead and bought  a pellet on it didn't actually stick to  using the peloton the thing is most of  these guys weren't enthusiasts bikers or  runners they were actually the type of  people who got a gym membership as a new  year's resolution and then only used it  five times throughout the entire year  and buying a peloton was basically their  cobot resolution many of them likely  felt that by spending a significant  amount of money and having live training  classes they would be more compelled to  work out and while this may have worked  for a few months or even several months  as people began to return to their  normal lives their fitness lives also  returned to being non-existent most of  these guys kept their pelotons around  for a few more months but eventually  they decided to sell them on ebay and  craigslist leading to these sites being  flooded with peloton listings not only  did these listings drive buying traffic  away from pelton's official website but  it sent a pretty strong signal that  everyday people who bought a peloton  didn't really find it that valuable  this isn't to say that peloton doesn't  make great products because their bikes  and treadmills are truly top of the line  these buyers were simply not part of the  target demographic and as they left the  scene peloton's revenue started to  stagnate and decline as pelotons started  to fall out of favor amongst everyday  consumers people and companies start to  poke fun at peloton ryan reynolds for  example hired the actress that played  the wife in the peloton commercial to  star in a gin commercial called the gift  that doesn't give back similarly just a  few months ago hbo aired an episode in  which a character called mr big suffers  from a heart attack and dies after  riding a peloton peloton shares would  fall 11.3 percent the day after this  episode aired on tv clearly peloton was  suffering from a fleeting customer base  and a shifting public image but it  didn't take long for things to go from  bad to worse  the nail in the coffin for peloton came  in march of 2021 when a video was  released of a child being sucked into  the back of a peloton treadmill and  dying following this story we saw dozens  of reports suggesting that adults  children and pets were injured due to  peloton products  honestly i'm not sure why peloton  received so much heat when a lot of the  fault is shared by the users of their  products nevertheless pelton implemented  a slew of software-based safety features  within the next month but this wasn't  enough to appease all the criticism they  were receiving from the media and in may  of 2021 peloton would finally give in  and issue a recall for their tread and  tread plus treadmills which is valid  till november of 2022 and the peloton  has been losing hundreds of millions per  quarter ever since i suspect that a lot  of the returns are just people cashing  in on an impulse purchase as opposed to  people returning these treadmills due to  legitimate safety concerns i guess it  was just these customers lucky day given  all this negative pr and the  oversaturated secondhand market peloton  was forced to slash prices to continue  appealing to customers which has just  made it even harder to turn a profit as  losses continue to pile up peloton has  been forced to make harder and harder  decisions one of these decisions came in  early february when peloton went ahead  and reduced the workforce by 2800  employees this was around the same time  that jon foley resigned from the company  and was replaced by barry mccarthy so  far john has lost his position as the  yoa and his status as a billionaire the  only question left is will jon also lose  his company  looking forward while pelton is  definitely in a terrible position right  now i think it's unlikely that they'll  file for bankruptcy and completely shut  down despite all their ups and downs  peloton has been able to grow a strong  base of core users that wouldn't use  anything else and just maintaining this  customer base could be quite lucrative  for peloton given their high  subscription fees with that being said  though the next few quarters don't look  that great as it's unlikely that peloton  can really start to build up positive  momentum again until the recall period  is over even after the recall is over  it's unlikely that peloton will ever  enjoy the same amount of interest and  excitement that they enjoyed at the peak  of the pandemic but over several years  it's possible that they're able to build  up a large enough base of enthusiasts  that they're able to pull in more  revenue and profit than ever before but  we'll just have to wait and see do you  guys think pelton will rise from the  ashes or do you think it will become an  infamous fitness fad comment that down  below also drop a like if you think  pelotons are overpriced and of course  consider checking out our international  channels to watch our videos in other  languages and consider subscribing to  see more questions logically answered  but until then i'm hari and i'll see you  guys on the next one  [Music]

